{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YaA1LCJUTBRuGqwHwBnBKy","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→{\n     2→  \"metadata\": {\n     3→    \"title\": \"The Cask of Amontillado - Poe\",\n     4→    \"author\": \"Edgar Allan Poe\",\n     5→    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/The Cask of Amontillado - Poe.txt\",\n     6→    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n     7→    \"total_word_count\": 2358,\n     8→    \"total_character_count\": 13023,\n     9→    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 15.693333333333333,\n    10→    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n    11→  },\n    12→  \"structure\": [\n    13→    {\n    14→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    15→      \"title\": null,\n    16→      \"index\": 1,\n    17→      \"start_position\": 24,\n    18→      \"end_position\": 13023,\n    19→      \"word_count\": 2354,\n    20→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 15.693333333333333,\n    21→      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    22→      \"children\": [],\n    23→      \"characters_present\": [\n    24→        \"Montresor\",\n    25→        \"Fortunato\"\n    26→      ],\n    27→      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolving to punish him with impunity during the chaotic carnival season. At dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit—into his family's catacombs under the pretense of tasting a rare pipe of Amontillado, exploiting Fortunato's pride in his wine expertise. As they descend through damp, bone-strewn vaults beneath the river, Montresor feigns concern for Fortunato's cough, offering wine to keep him compliant, until they reach a hidden niche where Montresor chains him to the wall and begins sealing him alive with bricks and mortar. Fortunato's initial laughter turns to desperate pleas, then silence, as Montresor completes the wall, leaving him entombed; the chapter ends with Montresor's chilling declaration that no one has disturbed the tomb in fifty years.\"\n    28→    }\n    29→  ],\n    30→  \"characters\": [\n    31→    {\n    32→      \"id\": \"char_fortunato_b564181f\",\n    33→      \"canonical_name\": \"Fortunato\",\n    34→      \"aliases\": [],\n    35→      \"role\": null,\n    36→      \"appearance\": null,\n    37→      \"personality\": null,\n    38→      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n    39→      \"descriptions\": [\n    40→        {\n    41→          \"text\": \"Fortunato is a prideful and intoxicated man whose expertise in wine becomes the instrument of his downfall. He is manipulated by Montresor under the guise of camaraderie, ultimately leading to his entombment in the catacombs. His initial confidence and laughter give way to desperate pleas and silence, revealing his vulnerability and the tragic consequence of his arrogance.\",\n    42→          \"source_position\": 88,\n    43→          \"chapter_index\": null,\n    44→          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n    45→        }\n    46→      ],\n    47→      \"relationships\": {},\n    48→      \"evidence\": [\n    49→        {\n    50→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato is unaware of Montresor's ill will despite outward displays of friendship\",\n    51→          \"quote\": \"It must be understood that neither by word nor deed had I given Fortunato cause to doubt my good will. I continued, as was my wont, to smile in his face, and he did not perceive that my smile now was at the thought of his immolation.\",\n    52→          \"position\": 722,\n    53→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    54→        },\n    55→        {\n    56→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato has a notable weakness in his pride regarding his wine expertise\",\n    57→          \"quote\": \"He had a weak point -- this Fortunato -- although in other regards he was a man to be respected and even feared. He prided himself on his connoisseurship in wine.\",\n    58→          \"position\": 921,\n    59→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    60→        },\n    61→        {\n    62→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato is deceived by Montresor's pretense of needing wine expertise\",\n    63→          \"quote\": \"I said to him --\\\"My dear Fortunato, you are luckily met. How remarkably well you are looking to-day. But I have received a pipe of what passes for Amontillado, and I have my doubts.\\\"\",\n    64→          \"position\": 1924,\n    65→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    66→        },\n    67→        {\n    68→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato is intoxicated and unaware of the danger as he follows Montresor\",\n    69→          \"quote\": \"Thus speaking, Fortunato possessed himself of my arm; and putting on a mask of black silk and drawing a roquelaire closely about my person, I suffered him to hurry me to my palazzo.\",\n    70→          \"position\": 3234,\n    71→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    72→        },\n    73→        {\n    74→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato's intoxication wears off, revealing his fear and awareness of danger\",\n    75→          \"quote\": \"I had scarcely laid the first tier of the masonry when I discovered that the intoxication of Fortunato had in a great measure worn off. The earliest indication I had of this was a low moaning cry from the depth of the recess. It was not the cry of a drunken man.\",\n    76→          \"position\": 10086,\n    77→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    78→        },\n    79→        {\n    80→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato initially laughs at the situation, showing disbelief and lack of understanding\",\n    81→          \"quote\": \"But now there came from out the niche a low laugh that erected the hairs upon my head. It was succeeded by a sad voice, which I had difficulty in recognizing as that of the noble Fortunato. The voice said--\\\"Ha! ha! ha! --he! he! he! --a very good joke, indeed --an excellent jest.\\\"\",\n    82→          \"position\": 11900,\n    83→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    84→        },\n    85→        {\n    86→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato pleads for mercy as he realizes his fate\",\n    87→          \"quote\": \"\\\"For the love of God, Montresor!\\\"\",\n    88→          \"position\": 12506,\n    89→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    90→        },\n    91→        {\n    92→          \"statement\": \"Fortunato's final cry is silenced, indicating his death\",\n    93→          \"quote\": \"There came forth in return only a jingling of the bells.\",\n    94→          \"position\": 12550,\n    95→          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    96→        }\n    97→      ],\n    98→      \"voice_notes\": null,\n    99→      \"first_appearance_chapter\": 1,\n   100→      \"mention_count\": 14,\n   101→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n   102→      \"is_narrator\": false,\n   103→      \"narrative_role\": null\n   104→    },\n   105→    {\n   106→      \"id\": \"char_montresor_dcb3c455\",\n   107→      \"canonical_name\": \"Montresor\",\n   108→      \"aliases\": [],\n   109→      \"role\": null,\n   110→      \"appearance\": null,\n   111→      \"personality\": null,\n   112→      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n   113→      \"descriptions\": [\n   114→        {\n   115→          \"text\": \"Montresor is the first-person narrator and perpetrator of a premeditated murder, driven by a desire for revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult. He manipulates Fortunato under the guise of wine connoisseurship, leading him into the family catacombs where he entombs him alive, demonstrating cold calculation, deception, and a lack of remorse, as evidenced by his chilling claim that the tomb remains undisturbed after fifty years.\",\n   116→          \"source_position\": 12364,\n   117→          \"chapter_index\": null,\n   118→          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n   119→        }\n   120→      ],\n   121→      \"relationships\": {},\n   122→      \"evidence\": [],\n   123→      \"voice_notes\": null,\n   124→      \"first_a